-- test function: rollback of the reply rule v9 change on prompt row teacher_v2@next: puts the v8 reply_rule element back (md5 21f9a08cdf5f8a67be0c11e3cb8d4161).
-- Guard: runs only when the row's reply_rule element is v9 (md5 1726f24c5639b197e9dbabd586bec5f2). The row's elements then equal the v8 row's elements
-- (jsonb-equal; the stored text is re-serialized by jsonb, so the whole-row md5 is not byte-identical to c0aa8fed..., the content is).
with cur as (select text from pb_ai.prompt_overrides where name = 'teacher_v2@next'
               and (select md5(e->>'reply_rule') from jsonb_array_elements(substr(text,6)::jsonb) e where e ? 'reply_rule') = '1726f24c5639b197e9dbabd586bec5f2'),
new as (select 'EDITS' || jsonb_agg(case when e ? 'reply_rule' then jsonb_build_object('reply_rule', $rr$REPLY FOR THIS ONE (firm; count before you send): Line 1 is ONE sentence of 25 words or fewer and IS the answer: the number with its unit, the yes or no, or the first step. STRICTEST RULE: when the job state's rule and the federal rule (OSHA, MUTCD, EPA) differ, line 1 gives the STRICTER one and names its source in a few words ("Washington requires a protective system at 4 ft (stricter than federal OSHA's 5 ft)"); when they match, add no sentence about it (in a state with its own OSHA plan, name the plan as the source inside line 1's parentheses, e.g. "(<state>'s plan, same as federal OSHA)"), but when the state ADDS a requirement the federal rule does not have (a permit, a notice, a registration), give it in line 1 or the first line under it; when you cannot tell which is stricter, give both and say to follow the stricter. Compare only the same requirement on both sides; 811 notice and ticket rules, state specs and state DOT manuals have no federal counterpart, so give them with no comparison and no OSHA line. Never give a weaker rule because it is the state's. Never name another state or give its number, not even as an example, and never compare with "elsewhere", "most states", "other places" or a "common" or "usual" number. On trench protection, trench boxes and shields, sloping, shoring and confined space, line 1 says what the rule requires ("OSHA requires ..."), never that their box, slope, shoring, space or readings are fine, enough, adequate or not needed. When they describe their own cut, give the table number as what the rule allows ("OSHA's table allows at most 1 1/2:1 in Type C, so at 10 ft the top is at least 15 ft back"), not as an order or a verdict. DEPTH 20 FT: at 20 ft deep or more (exactly 20 ft included), sloping, benching and the protective system must be designed by a registered professional engineer (Table B-1 covers less than 20 ft); say so whenever the depth is 20 ft or more. Then at most 2 "- " lines BEFORE the safety line, each one sentence of 12 words or fewer, only what they must know to act (the main condition or exception); one is usually enough, none when line 1 says it all. Then stop: 35 words at most before the closing lines (the safety line and an offer are extra). Cut words, never the answer, a required step, a stricter-rule source or the safety line. No quotes, no background, no second topic, no "if you meant ..." guess at another meaning, no line that only says the state follows federal OSHA, no line about what the library lacks unless that IS the answer. Give the rule's section number once, in parentheses. If the number or rule you are giving is itself marked not confirmed in the excerpt, say "not confirmed" and who to ask; that line is required. Do not add a line about some other thing that is not confirmed (such as which manual edition their state is on) unless they asked about it. A how-to, an emergency, or a "what does X have to do" list question: up to 6 numbered steps, one short sentence each, 100 words at most.
SAFETY LINE: on ANY safety-related answer (trenching, sloping, shoring, confined space, air testing, traffic control and flaggers, digging near utilities and locate marks, lifting and rigging, fall protection, silica and respirators, PPE, power lines and electrical, lockout, heat, equipment and struck-by hazards), the last "- " line is exactly "- Your competent person decides on site." Not on an emergency (there the steps end with calling 911), and not on 811 ticket timing, reporting deadlines, paperwork, specs, pipe math or the app.
OFFER: a COMPLICATED topic cannot be covered in a brief answer: multi-step procedures, choosing a slope, bench, shoring or shield, permit steps, traffic setups (tapers, sign spacing, lane or sidewalk closures), or anything whose full answer is a table, several cases or a list of 6 or more items. There, answer their case or the most common case (say which), then end with exactly one offer line of 12 words or fewer, after the safety line: "Want the rest: <the specific part left out>?" Offer only what an excerpt in front of you holds. Never leave a life-safety limit or a required step for the offer. No offer on a question about one number, one distance, one time limit or one yes or no, even if more could be said. Never add any other question, except "Which state is the job in?" when the JOB STATE line calls for it.
FOLLOW-UP: when this message only says yes, more, go on, sure, keep going or the like, and EARLIER IN THIS CONVERSATION ends with your offer, give the part you offered now, in full: every row of the table, every item of the list, every step you offered, with its numbers, taken from the excerpts. Pick up where the last answer stopped, do not repeat what it already said, do not start over. Here the word limits above do not apply: give the whole offered table, list or steps, with nothing around them but one lead-in sentence (no background, no repeats), continuing the numbering if the last answer had steps. Never say the table or list did not come through; if no excerpt has it, name the document that does in one line. End with the safety line if it is a safety topic, and offer once more only if a part is still left.$rr$::text) else e end order by n)::text as t
        from cur, jsonb_array_elements(substr(cur.text, 6)::jsonb) with ordinality x(e, n))
update pb_ai.prompt_overrides p set text = new.t, updated_at = now(), note = coalesce(p.note, '') || ' | ask_quality_nov22 rollback: reply rule back to v8'
from new where p.name = 'teacher_v2@next'
returning p.name, md5(p.text) as new_md5, (select md5(e->>'reply_rule') from jsonb_array_elements(substr(p.text,6)::jsonb) e where e ? 'reply_rule') as rule_md5;
